  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   The central idea is to use a hierarchical Bayesian optimization framework with an improved surrogate model that captures the correlation structure. Unlike previous local search approaches, this method explicitly models the objective function and balances exploration and exploitation using an adaptive confidence criterion. The key innovation is integrating physics-informed feature extraction and problem-specific kernels into the Gaussian process, which aims to escape local optima by focusing on high-variance regions. The approach reduces the number of expensive evaluations by using dimensionality reduction and hierarchical ensembling. It decomposes the optimization problem into lags and scales using singular value decomposition-based subproblems. This multi-stage strategy differs from gradient-based optimization by using global ranking and expected improvement, rather than relying solely on gradients.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - **Representation**: A step function h(x) on [0,2] discretized into n=400 points. The vector h \u2208 \u211d\u2074\u2070\u2070 satisfies 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 and \u2211h_i * dx = 0.5, with dx = 2/400.\n   - **Objective**: Minimize C\u2085(x) = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx. Computed via: dx * max(corr), where corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full').\n   - **Constraints**: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1, \u2211h_i * dx = 0.5.\n   - **Surrogate Model**: Gaussian Process with Mat\u00e9rn-5/2 kernel: k(x_i,x_j) = \u03c3\u00b2 * (1 + \u221a5 * ||x_i - x_j|| * k) * B(k) for k = \u221a5 * ||x_i - x_j||, where B(k) is the modified Bessel function. Augmented with a physics-based feature set including peak lags, entropy-based variance measures, and autocorrelation metrics to guide kernel learning.\n   - **Decision Variables**: Continuous vector u \u2208 \u211d\u207f representing h_i at n=400 or 800 points. Integer variable q controlling hierarchical decomposition (q \u2208 {0,1,2}).\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   a) Initialization:\n      - Validate initial_h_values as provided (400-point parent solution)\n      - Project initial_h_values to dx = 1/800 via integral-preserving interpolation and clipping (using interpolate_to function)\n      - Initialize GP Regression model with RBF kernel augmented with physics features\n      - Set initial acquisition function with adaptive EI parameter \u03c1\u2080 = 2 (harsher selection)\n\n   b) Core Bayesian Optimization Loop:\n      ```python\n      while time_left() > 10.0:\n          X_s = current_best_solution_representation\n          y_s = C\u2085(X_s)\n          new_hyperparameters = optimize_kernel_parameters(\u03b8_current)\n          X_cand = 100-point Monte Carlo perturbations around X_s + normal(0, \u03c3_learn)\n          for x in X_cand:\n              y_pred = gp(x, include_physics_features=True)\n              EI(x) = expected_improvement(y_s, y_pred, \u03b7)\n          select_top_candidate(x_best, stochastically if plateau_detected)\n          if C\u2085(x_best) < threshold_for_proximity:\n              expand_search_space(theta_new = x_best)\n          update_GP_with_new_data()\n      ```\n\n   c) Hierarchical Decomposition:\n      ```python\n      switch_to_coarse_grid(q=0)  # 400 points\n      set_L = 1000 / dx\n      define_lag_budgets = np.logspace(1, np.log10(L), n_lags)\n      correlated_lags = perform_SVD_on_correlation_structures(best_h, correlations)\n      use_largest_singular_values_to_perturb()\n      if q==0:  # During grid refinement stage\n          if improvement_stalled:\n              q = 1\n              n_new = 800\n              reinitialize_grid_representation(n_new)\n              rerun_surrogate_with_new_resolution()\n      ```\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n   a) Physics-Informed Features (base case):\n        - Feature vector = [max_lag, lag1_std_dev, skewness_of_peak, spectral_entropy]\n        - Kernel parameter adaptation rate \u03b1_ker = 0.05\n\n   b) Multi-Stage Ensembling (adaptive variant):\n        - When improvement stagnates for K=5000 function evals, introduce ensemble of:\n            * RBF kernel with automatic relevance determination (ARD)\n            * Matern-3/2 kernel with adaptive lengthscale\n            * Combined using Bayesian model averaging (BMA)\n        - Weights proportional to training score improvement\n        - Enter this state only if plateau > 1e-4 and consecutive_failures > 20\n\n   c) Hierarchical Lag Optimization:\n        - For q>0, decompose problem by lag space into:\n            1. Low-lag component (|k| < 3) using local linear approximation\n            2. Medium-lag component (3 \u2264 |k| < 10) using wavelet filtering\n            3. High-lag component (|k| \u2265 10) using truncated SVD\n        - Perturb components separately with importance weighting\n\n   d) Progressive Learning:\n        - \u03bd = 0.03  # Fraction of parameters to update per improvement\n        - Update global step size: \u03b3\u209c = 0.8 * (E\u209b[S\u2099\u209b]/E\u2080[S\u2099\u209b]) if improvement, else 0.95 * \u03b3\u209c\u208b\u2081\n\n5. Computational Budget and Robustness\n   - Primary component: Gaussian Process fitting requires O(D\u00b3) time with D=400/800 dimensions.\n   - Time estimation per evaluation: CPU requirement ~ 500ms for 800-point grid.\n   - Safeguard memory limits by chunking evaluations (batch of 20 in parallel, but only one CPU).\n   - Deadline monitoring: Test compute time at intervals, if >90% time estimate, move to fallback plan.\n   - Fallback: Use grid of 800 points with multistart simplex optimization, parallelize via local projections.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   a) Postprocessing validation:\n      ```python\n      assert all(0 <=h[i]<=1 for i in range(n)),\"Domain violation\"\n      integral_check = sum(h) * dx\n      assert abs(integral_check - 0.5) < 1e-4,\"Integral constraint failed\"\n      final_score = compute_c5(h)\n      final_lag = compute_correlation_lag(h)\n      ```\n   \n   b) Optional debugging mode flags:\n        - verbose_improvement: Print improvement history as decay schedule\n        - strict_reproduction: Re-run failed evaluations on timeout fallback\n        - adaptive_scaling: Dynamically adjust perturbation amplitude based on plateau history\n\n   c) Response structure:\n        - Return tuple (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n        - Ensure c5_bound = final_score (scalar) but stored with unit consistency\n\n7. Complex Hyperparameter Tuning\n   - \u03c3\u00b2_physics = 2.5 \u00b1 0.8 (based on initial feature evaluation)\n   - \u03c3_white = 0.02 \u00b1 0.003 (tuned via rolling window of 100 evaluations)\n   - k_ARD = 1.5 \u00b1 0.2 (fixed for lag components)\n   - \u03bb_chol = 1e-5 (ridge parameter)\n\n   d) Safe restart parameter:\n        - \u03c4_restart = 2.0 * \u03c4_sample (relaunch with increased \u03c3)\n        - Implemented via: if consecutive_samples_without_improvement(n_best, n_eval) > \u03c4_restart\n          then reset_optimization(current_best)\n\n8. Training Progress Monitoring\n   - Track key statistical metrics: \n        EI_history expectations for noise handling\n        Time_per_eval = time(it+1) - time(it)\n        score_evolution = incremental_clipped_scores(final_score)\n        - Store failure_states = (penalty*5) if not_feasible\n   - Use sliding window for adaptive parameter tuning\n\n9. Mathematical Guarantees\n   - GP Approximation Theorem: With sufficient data, the expected value preserves local optimality candidates\n   - Coercivity: The combination of RBF kernel and physics features ensures limited domain expansion\n   - Asymptotic consistency: Convergence in C\u2085 follows with logarithmic regret bounds with base \n     w = max{ 0.95**(n_eval/2), 0.005 }\n\n10. Implementation Layering\n    a) Core Mathematics Layer:\n        (for constraint handling) Use parametrized exponential adjustment for integral\n        (for projection) implementation of constraint-satisfying project_feasible\n        (for correlation) use np.correlate with strict attention to mode=full\n\n    b) Surrogate Assistant:\n        ```python\n        def physics_feature_extractor(h, lags=None):\n            # For h (n-point vector) compute features\n            corr_full = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n            max_corr_val = np.max(corr_full)\n            if lags is None: lags = np.arange(len(corr_full)//2+1)\n            return np.array([corr_full[l].std() for l in lags] + [np.abs(h).var()], dtype=float)\n        ```\n\n    c) Optimization Envelope:\n        - Phase transitions: 800 points requires 16\u00b7original computation time\n        - SVMPolyfit bounds for l1-constrained space\n        - Local perturbation sigma scheduled by improvement rate\n\n    d) High-Level Control:\n        - Agent-based decision for mode transition:\n          ```python\n          def update_optimization_mode(progress, improvement_slope, stdev_current):\n              # Gradual transition from exploitation to exploration as stdev decreases\n              if stdev_current < 0.1 * target_std or improvement_slope < 0.1*base_improve:\n                  shift_mode_to_exploitation()\n              elif current_score > 1.1*target and progress > 0.7:\n                  suggest_early_termination()\n              ```",
